    not_duplicate_of: []  # c84b
    pr_ref:  # c84b
    description: "Filed 2026-04-22 after WI-hizik landed the drift-guard baseline. The baseline grandfathered 419 io_primitives entries that do not appear in taint_sinks; this item asks for a human decision on how to reconcile them.\n\nContext\n-------\n- WI-hizik froze current drift via _taint_sink_drift_baseline.yaml so growth fails the test. It did NOT reconcile existing drift.\n- ADR-0017 explicitly separates the two catalogs (taint_sinks is designed for project-local extension). The separation rationale holds for user-contributed catalogs, but does not defend 419 first-party primitives being absent from the first-party taint_sinks catalog.\n\nBreakdown of the 419\n--------------------\n                 Python  Rust  Go  Java  TS    TOTAL\n  fs_write       31      22    38   49   40      180\n  net_send       57      24    50   53   55      239\n  TOTAL          88      46    88  102   95      419\n\nSemantic model mismatch (root cause)\n------------------------------------\nThe two catalogs encode different models:\n- io_primitives = 'if this function is called directly, it is an IO boundary' → enumerates every syscall-level primitive so io-boundaries chain-tracing bottoms out at the right place.\n- taint_sinks = 'if tainted data reaches this function, it is dangerous' → written as a minimal 'reference' set of highest-level APIs per language.\nFor soundness, taint_sinks should be at least as comprehensive as io_primitives (otherwise calling http.client.HTTPConnection.request directly is a silent taint-flow gap). So the target state is: taint_sinks ⊇ io_primitives fs_write/net_send, minus deliberate exceptions.\n\nWhat is missing (concrete findings)\n-----------------------------------\na. **Destructive fs_write (31 entries across 5 langs):** rmtree, rm_rf, remove, rmdir, unlink, delete, Remove, Delete. HIGH priority — these are exactly the sinks you want tainted-data checks on.\n\nb. **fs_write metadata-change (20):** chmod, chown, touch, utime. Meaningful — if tainted data decides whether to chmod 0600 vs 0777, that's a flow worth catching.\n\nc. **Major HTTP client libraries missing from net_send (huge gap):** requests (Python), axios/fetch/got/ky/superagent/undici/node-fetch (TypeScript), httpx (Python), aiohttp (Python), Apache HttpClient (Java), OkHttp (Java), Retrofit (Java), reqwest (Rust), hyper (Rust), gin/echo Response writers (Go). Covered by io_primitives, ignored by taint_sinks. This is the largest reconciliation impact.\n\nd. **Browser-storage sinks (debatable, 6 entries):** io_primitives/javascript.yaml classifies localStorage.setItem / sessionStorage.setItem / localStorage.clear etc. as fs_write. Whether these should also be taint sinks is genuinely a judgment call: they are attacker-accessible via XSS but not host filesystem. Recommend: keep exempted in the baseline with an explicit rationale comment.\n\ne. **Syscall-level entries (Go syscall.*, unix.*; Python os.* low-level):** ~12 entries. Rarely called directly; most code uses the stdlib wrappers. Could be exempted with 'covered by higher-level wrapper' rationale.\n\nf. **stdlib serialization writes:** json.dump, pickle.dump, csv.writer. Currently only 'builtins.open' is a taint sink. But these ARE classic taint sinks — if tainted data reaches pickle.dump, you care.\n\ng. **Network primitives beyond HTTP:** ftplib (3), smtplib (2), socket.sendmsg (1), asyncio streams (5). Non-HTTP protocols still carry data over networks.\n\nRecommendation — three-tier reconciliation\n------------------------------------------\nPR 1 (small, high confidence): Port the 31 destructive fs_write entries + the common HTTP client libraries (requests, httpx, axios, fetch, reqwest, OkHttp, Apache HttpClient, aiohttp). Roughly 80-100 entries, near-zero ambiguity. Baseline shrinks to ~320 grandfather entries.\n\nPR 2 (per-language batch): Work through Python, Rust, Go, Java, TypeScript one at a time. Each PR adds the language's stdlib write/send primitives to taint_sinks, updates baseline. Expected 4-5 PRs each removing 40-80 grandfather entries. No architectural changes.\n\nPR 3 (policy decisions needed): Decide what to do with (a) browser-storage sinks — tainted localStorage is a real XSS concern, but 'taint sink' is currently framed as host_filesystem or network, so a new zone 'browser_storage' may be needed; (b) syscall-level entries — explicit 'covered by higher-level wrapper' exemption with rationale comment.\n\nQuestions for human\n-------------------\n1. Is 'taint_sinks should be at least as comprehensive as io_primitives write/send' the right target? Or is there a design intent that taint_sinks stays minimal and project-local catalogs fill the gap on a per-project basis?\n2. Do you want PR 1 (destructive + HTTP clients) as one unit, or split further?\n3. Browser-storage taint sinks — introduce a 'browser_storage' trust zone or leave exempt?\n4. Syscall-level entries — exempt or port?\n5. Reverse-direction drift (entries in taint_sinks not in io_primitives; 32 entries counted at WI-hizik time): worth a separate item or fold in here?\n\nScope boundary for this item\n----------------------------\nThis item proposes architecture/policy. Actual YAML edits belong to child items (PR 1, PR 2, PR 3). Status: needs_human_review — awaiting decisions above before creating concrete child items."  # c84b
    fields: {}  # c84b
