- op: discuss  # a5df
  at: '2026-04-22T08:35:52Z'  # a5df
  by: agent  # a5df
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a5df
  clock: 3  # a5df
  nonce: a5df  # a5df
  message: "Human triage on 2026-04-22. Decisions from conversation:\n\nQ1 — yes, taint_sinks should be at least as comprehensive as io_primitives write/send, but implemented via AUTO-IMPORT WITH OVERRIDE rather than manual reconciliation. io_primitives becomes the single source of truth for primitive enumeration. Every io_primitives write-side entry is automatically treated as a taint_sinks entry with a default classification. User taint_sinks entries matching by (module, name, kind) override the auto-derived default. 'Reading A' (paranoid default, user overrides narrow): auto-derived entry is a sink for ANY taint label at trust_level=untrusted, zone determined by io_primitives category.\n\nDefault zone mapping (confirmed):\n  fs_write → host_fs\n  net_send → network\n  subprocess → host_fs\n  env_write → host_env (new zone)\n  ipc_send → ipc (new zone)\n  browser_storage_write → browser_storage (new zone, see Q3)\n\nQ2 — moot under auto-import. No 419-entry manual reconciliation needed. One implementation PR instead: build the auto-import loader in taint.py, add the new zone enum values, remove the shipped first-party taint_sinks/*.yaml entries that duplicate io_primitives (keep project-agnostic overrides only).\n\nQ3 — option 3 (structurally best): restructure io_primitives to introduce a new browser_storage_write category. Move localStorage.setItem / sessionStorage.setItem / localStorage.clear / sessionStorage.clear / localStorage.removeItem / sessionStorage.removeItem out of javascript.yaml#fs_write into javascript.yaml#browser_storage_write. Add browser_storage_write to boundary_types in io_boundary.py. Add browser_storage zone to taint.py. Scope to write-side only; read-side (localStorage.getItem, sessionStorage.getItem, document.cookie reads) is asymmetric follow-up work.\n\nQ4 — moot under auto-import reading A. Syscall-level entries in Go/Python/Rust become auto-derived sinks by default; users exempt per-project if noisy.\n\nQ5 — largely moot. Under auto-import, 'entries in taint_sinks but not io_primitives' = legitimate project-local sinks (PlazaFlow CRDT relay, VPN TUN interface, etc). Nothing to reconcile.\n\nFollow-on consequences:\n\n1. WI-hizik's drift-guard test + _taint_sink_drift_baseline.yaml become redundant once auto-import lands. The baseline becomes a historical artifact. My lean: tear down as part of the auto-import PR with a CHANGELOG note. Keep a copy of the baseline in git history so it's recoverable as a one-time reference.\n\n2. WI-jojis scope needs a decision: narrow (extend TaintSource data model to support attribute kind, ship host_env taint_sources catalog manually) or broad (implement auto-import directly, which supersedes the narrow data-model extension). If auto-import reads IoPrimitive objects directly, the taint data model may not even need its own attribute-kind support. Raising with human before starting WI-jojis implementation.\n\n3. Symmetry question for future work: should auto-import apply to the READ side of io_primitives as well? io_primitives.env_read → taint_sources at label=host_secret, io_primitives.net_recv → taint_sources at label=untrusted_input, etc. This mirrors the write-side design. If yes, WI-jojis's original goal (ship host_env taint_sources catalog) is subsumed by symmetric auto-import. Noting as future item; not deciding here.\n\nStatus transitioned needs_human_review → todo_hard. Ready to decompose into implementation sub-items once WI-jojis scope question is resolved."  # a5df
